WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:07.819
Let's go back to the phones at 6 1 7 8 3 0
47 50 and see what is on people's minds.

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:11.380
Starting with our caller from the 2 4 0 area
code.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.360
Who's calling today from 2 4 0.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.490
Where are you calling from?

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.400
Thank you, Jason.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.939
David, this is Rob.

00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:20.179
I'm calling from Maryland.

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:23.090
Hey, Rob, what's up?

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:34.930
So I have I've always worked at small businesses,
I've always resisted going to big corporate

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:40.719
places for work because I know that I would
I would suffer it would be spirit crushing.

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:50.840
I prefer places where rather than there being
a very established culture, the place has

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:59.190
a malleable personality that I can actually
come in and contribute to, OK?

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.920
And big because I work for those types of
places.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:10.650
I'm unemployed right now and and it's looking
less and less likely that those types of places

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:18.899
are going to come back after the dust settles
because, you know, they they typically have

00:01:18.899 --> 00:01:25.350
much lower margins that they're running on,
that if they miss a month of profit, they

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.670
they can't keep up.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:35.479
And so now I'm very reluctantly applying to,
you know, the Safeways and the c_v_s_ is and

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:42.500
the Burger Kings of the world, because that's
all that's going to be left after this.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:49.360
And we're also kind of creating an entire
generation of, you know, the young people

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.539
right now who are seeing that they really
only have three kind of options as far as

00:01:55.539 --> 00:01:56.840
career paths.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:02.060
If they want their careers to be pandemic
proof, they can either go into public service

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.729
like government healthcare or education.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:11.019
They can go into they can go work for

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:21.040
massive fast food chain or at a grocery store,
or they can get one of those corporate jobs

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.650
where they can work from home.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:23.650
And those are diminishing.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.650
Lee, rare.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:30.140
I mean, those are the jobs of our parents
generation, not ours.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:40.370
And so we are going to do it for for a long
time, have this shift.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:49.100
I think that that crushes the dreams of a
lot of people who may have been better in

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:57.830
nonessential fields such as cosmetology or
the arts or culinary or, you know, being in

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:04.750
a research field of academia where you have
to actually go somewhere to study something.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:12.700
And I want to know just if you had any ideas
about how.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:24.260
How we could incentivize where it or if this
is even a good thing, like should unnecessary

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.420
jobs just.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.000
Disappear.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.860
And if not, how?

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.470
How do we incentivize people to still

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.230
follow their dreams?

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.250
So there's a lot there, Rob.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.790
And so, first of all, I don't know that I
agree with you about the jobs that are pandemic

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:43.790
proof.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:49.189
I mean, we just learned this week about the
nearly 5 percent GDP reduction in the first

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:50.599
quarter.

00:03:50.599 --> 00:03:55.790
And half of that GDP reduction was in health
care, which is counterintuitive because with

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:58.890
so many coronavirus patients, what how could
that be?

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.159
It's because almost all non-emergency health
care has been shut down.

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:09.909
And it certainly has not been pandemic proof
for people who work in dermatology offices

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:10.909
and the like.

00:04:10.909 --> 00:04:13.250
So I don't totally agree with the premise.

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:19.030
But what I would tell you is just because
a job is not essential in a pandemic doesn't

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:20.880
mean it's not an essential job.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:27.320
So as an example, transportation is essential
to ninety nine point nine nine percent of

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.910
human existence.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:29.910
We're in a pandemic

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.610
shut down where people aren't traveling.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:37.880
So everything from from cab drivers to airline
pilots are suffering right now.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.670
But I would not want to disincentivize people
from working in those fields because they're

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:42.670
so important.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.590
Ninety nine point nine, nine percent of the
time.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.800
What we need to do is we need to be Morse
take be more proactive.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.300
As I spoke about on the Wednesday show this
week about softening the blow to a lot of

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.820
these industries, and by that I mean to the
workers not did like the hedge fund people

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.970
that are speculating in these businesses.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.980
We need to have an economic system that's
more proactively protecting them.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.750
But the last thing I would say is, hey, during
however long this says if it's two months,

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:16.470
if it's nine, whatever it is to say there
were jobs that weren't really useful during

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.010
that time.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:22.260
These are jobs that are absolutely necessary
to to society, ninety nine point nine nine

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.260
percent of the time.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.531
And I would not suggest people abandon them.

00:05:24.531 --> 00:05:27.230
We just need to be better prepared to deal
with situations like this.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.130
My friend, I'm going to let you go because
I have so many people that that want to get

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.130
on.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.490
But it's an important question and I'm glad
that you asked it.

